WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.266
Running us

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:00.967
right now is

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.602
James Cameron Diver, Explorer,

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.037
director of The Abyss,

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:04.471
a deep sea

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:05.472
thriller, and most famously,

00:00:05.472 --> 00:00:06.272
of course, Titanic,

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:07.807
as well as well as many other

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:08.708
great films.

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:09.876
James, I appreciate you joining us.

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:11.011
Someone who's devoted his life

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:11.711
to exploration,

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:12.178
as you have

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:13.713
in many ways under the sea and wondering

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:16.216
what is going through your mind tonight.

00:00:16.216 --> 00:00:17.283
Well, thanks, Anderson.

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:18.485
I mean, obviously,

00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:19.519
we're all

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:21.521
we're all kind of heartsick

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:23.523
from the outcome of this.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:25.125
And I've been living with it

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:26.393
for a few days now

00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:27.660
as some of my other colleagues

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.963
in the deep submergence community

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.831
I was out on a ship myself

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.600
when the event happened on Sunday.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.367
The first I heard of

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:35.835
it was Monday morning.

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:38.571
I immediately got on my network

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:40.673
because it's a very small community

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:42.542
in the deep submergence group

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.645
and found out some

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:47.614
information within about a half hour

00:00:47.614 --> 00:00:49.349
that they had lost comms

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:50.016
and they had lost

00:00:50.016 --> 00:00:52.018
tracking simultaneously.

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:53.219
The only scenario

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:54.120
that I could come up with

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.722
in my mind that could account for

00:00:55.722 --> 00:00:59.159
that was it was an implosion, a shockwave

00:00:59.159 --> 00:01:00.160
event so powerful

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:00.693
that it

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:02.729
actually took out a secondary system

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:04.230
that has its own

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.665
its own pressure vessel

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:07.200
and its own battery power supply,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.101
which is the

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:10.703
transponder that the ship uses to track

00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:11.971
where the sub is.

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.540
So I was thinking implosion then.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.008
That's Monday morning.

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:16.209
I got on the horn again

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:17.610
with some other people,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.045
tracked down some intel

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:21.214
that was probably of a military origin,

00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:22.816
although it could have been research

00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:23.883
because there are hydrophones

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:25.585
all over the Atlantic

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:26.986
and got confirmation

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:29.155
that there was some kind of loud noise

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.191
consistent with an implosion event.

00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:31.991
That seemed to me

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:33.093
enough confirmation

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:35.295
that I let all of my inner

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:36.930
circle of people know that we

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.065
had lost our comrades.

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.067
And I

00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:42.735
encourage everybody to

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:44.504
to raise a glass in their honor

00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:45.872
on Monday.

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:48.541
Then I watched over the ensuing days

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:50.410
this whole sort of everybody

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:51.811
running around with their hair on fire

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:52.946
search,

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:53.546
knowing full

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:54.914
well that it was futile,

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:55.949
hoping against hope

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:57.217
that I was wrong,

00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:59.719
but knowing in my bones that I wasn't.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.588
And so it certainly

00:02:01.588 --> 00:02:04.023
wasn't a surprise today.

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:05.992
And I just feel terrible for the families

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:07.794
that had to go through all of these

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:10.663
false hopes that kept getting dangled,

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:13.833
you know, as it played out.

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:18.271
You've I just want to to

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:19.739
you said it was on Monday

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:20.607
that you learned

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:22.509
of these these

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:23.343
listening devices

00:02:23.343 --> 00:02:24.577
underwater had picked up

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:26.880
the sound of an implosion

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:27.814
or what was believed.

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:30.783
Yeah, that's that's as it came to me.

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:33.153
Now, that's hearsay. Multiple, you know,

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:35.855
they were credible sources.

00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:38.825
So, you know,

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:40.727
I took that as a factor

00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:41.895
that I multiply it in

00:02:41.895 --> 00:02:42.962
with the other factors.

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:43.863
And I couldn't

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:45.965
think of any other scenario.

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:47.433
In which a sub would be lost

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:49.502
where it lost comms and navigation

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:52.105
at the same time and stayed out of touch

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.040
and did not surface.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.041
I was also told,

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:56.676
and I don't have confirmation on this,

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:58.678
that they had they were on descent.

00:02:58.678 --> 00:03:00.180
There were a couple hundred meters

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.481
above the sea floor

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:03.316
and they dropped their weights.

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:05.518
Now, the only way for the ship to know

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:06.219
that they had dropped

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:06.920
their ascent way

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.022
to be an emergency aboard

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:10.757
is if they had called that in,

00:03:10.757 --> 00:03:12.492
that they were they were ascending.

00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:13.293
So I,

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:16.829
I believe now that they had some warning

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:17.430
that they heard

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.300
some acoustic signature of the hull

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.901
beginning to laminate.

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:23.102
An investigation

00:03:23.102 --> 00:03:26.139
will hopefully eventually show what

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:27.240
what did happen

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.975
because we all need to know

00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:30.376
as we go forward, the deep

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.512
submergence community needs to know

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:33.713
exactly what happened

00:03:36.316 --> 00:03:37.850
You've made dozens

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:39.252
of I mean, just extraordinary

00:03:39.252 --> 00:03:40.453
deepwater expeditions,

00:03:40.453 --> 00:03:41.888
including more than 32.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:43.723
The Titanic cell itself

00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:44.724
you've also gone

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:46.659
far deeper than the 13,000 feet

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:47.594
where the Titanic is.

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:48.628
I think you've gone

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:49.162
deeper than just

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:50.964
about anybody into the ocean.

00:03:50.964 --> 00:03:53.733
I wrote it into

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:55.335
I forgot the name of the place

00:03:55.335 --> 00:03:56.703
you went, the challenger deep.

00:03:56.703 --> 00:03:57.770
Which Challenger did

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:00.106
you went in your own design.

00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:00.373
Yeah.

00:04:00.373 --> 00:04:02.342
You went in your own designed craft.

00:04:02.342 --> 00:04:03.443
That's a submersible

00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:04.410
that was experimental

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:04.711
and didn't

00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:05.812
go through

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:06.713
the sort of the standard

00:04:06.713 --> 00:04:07.580
safety protocols.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:08.781
But the difference is

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:10.817
you were not taking passengers on board.

00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:13.152
Would you ever have taken passengers

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:16.389
on board a submersible that had not gone

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:17.357
through the standard

00:04:17.357 --> 00:04:19.525
maritime safety protocols?

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:20.426
No, not at all.

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:22.228
I mean, my sub that I went to the

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:22.962
to the Challenger

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:25.265
Deep dove safely three times deeper than

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:26.599
then Titanic.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:28.001
We made

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:30.003
multiple dives in that in that sub.

00:04:31.571 --> 00:04:33.640
That sub was a single seater

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:36.542
and it was only contemplated that myself

00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:37.744
and the engineer

00:04:37.744 --> 00:04:38.878
with whom I co-designed

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:40.413
the vehicle would be the only

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:41.547
pilot of that sub.

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:43.049
And we worked on it for seven years.

00:04:43.049 --> 00:04:45.285
We knew every detail of it intimately.

00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:45.952
I was involved

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:47.520
in every phase of the testing.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.523
So, you know, I assessed the risks.

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:51.724
I understood them very well.

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:52.859
And those were risks

00:04:52.859 --> 00:04:54.060
I was willing to take.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.763
I would never take it upon myself

00:04:56.763 --> 00:04:57.697
to ask someone else

00:04:57.697 --> 00:05:00.033
to take that type of risk.

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:01.134
And if I were designing

00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:02.635
a multi seat vehicle

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:03.569
where I intended to be

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:04.337
the pilot,

00:05:04.337 --> 00:05:05.538
we'd go through

00:05:05.538 --> 00:05:07.707
all of the rigorous test protocols

00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:08.875
and review protocols

00:05:08.875 --> 00:05:09.375
that you have

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:10.977
with, let's say, Airbus,

00:05:10.977 --> 00:05:13.112
which is the American Bureau of Shipping

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:15.715
or DMV or German Lloyd's,

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:16.949
who are the major

00:05:18.117 --> 00:05:20.019
bureaus that the Class

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:20.453
A sub,

00:05:20.453 --> 00:05:21.754
they call it classing,

00:05:21.754 --> 00:05:24.257
but it's basically certification.

00:05:24.257 --> 00:05:25.692
And I think it was unconscionable

00:05:25.692 --> 00:05:27.493
that that this group

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:29.829
did not go through that rigorous process

00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:34.400
What is you know, this was a

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:35.468
an experimental design.

00:05:35.468 --> 00:05:36.536
There's no question about it.

00:05:36.536 --> 00:05:39.205
It's a carbon composite.

00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:40.540
You can tell us more about what

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:41.374
that actually means.

00:05:41.374 --> 00:05:41.808
I mean, it's

00:05:41.808 --> 00:05:43.142
the kind of stuff used in spacecraft,

00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:44.610
but is it designed

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.346
for deep underwater pressure?

00:05:46.346 --> 00:05:47.413
And what is the

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:48.481
what is the danger

00:05:48.481 --> 00:05:49.816
of that kind of material

00:05:49.816 --> 00:05:52.085
in this kind of environment?

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:54.020
It's completely inappropriate for

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.956
for a vessel that sees external pressure.

00:05:58.291 --> 00:05:58.925
You know,

00:05:58.925 --> 00:06:00.393
carbon fiber composites are used

00:06:00.393 --> 00:06:02.128
very very successfully

00:06:02.128 --> 00:06:03.830
for internal pressure, pressure

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:05.898
vessels like, let's say, a scuba tank.

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.834
And you can get two or three times

00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:08.634
multiple of what

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:09.736
you could get out of steel

00:06:09.736 --> 00:06:10.703
or aluminum

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:13.306
for for that type of pressure bottle.

00:06:13.606 --> 00:06:14.340
But for something

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:15.742
that's seeing external pressure,

00:06:15.742 --> 00:06:17.343
all of the advantages of composite

00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:18.144
materials

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:19.245
go away

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:21.481
and all the disadvantages come into play.

00:06:21.814 --> 00:06:23.916
So if you're using a uniform material

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:28.321
like steel or titanium or ceramic

00:06:28.321 --> 00:06:29.222
or acrylic,

00:06:30.323 --> 00:06:30.923
you can do

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:31.891
computer modeling

00:06:31.891 --> 00:06:33.726
with a high degree of accuracy

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:34.827
and confidence.

00:06:34.827 --> 00:06:36.129
The second you start

00:06:36.129 --> 00:06:37.597
doing carbon composite

00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:39.699
or any kind of composite materials,

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:41.267
you're introducing to materials

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:43.469
that are in contact with each other,

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:44.837
the filament itself,

00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:46.773
and then the epoxy matrix

00:06:46.773 --> 00:06:48.574
that it that it sits within.

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:49.275
And at that point

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:51.477
you have degradation failure.

00:06:51.477 --> 00:06:53.913
So we always understood

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:55.381
that this was the wrong material

00:06:55.381 --> 00:06:56.883
for submersible hulls,

00:06:56.883 --> 00:06:59.519
because with each pressure cycle,

00:06:59.719 --> 00:07:01.788
you can have progressive damage.

00:07:01.788 --> 00:07:03.689
So it's quite insidious

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:04.357
because you may have

00:07:04.357 --> 00:07:06.225
a number of successful dives,

00:07:06.225 --> 00:07:07.560
which is what happened here,

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.562
and then have it fail later.

00:07:09.562 --> 00:07:11.130
If I were diving in a sub

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:12.598
that that was fully certified,

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:13.633
I wouldn't think about it.

00:07:13.633 --> 00:07:15.034
But even in my own sub,

00:07:15.034 --> 00:07:16.769
which had a steel hull,

00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:17.970
I knew that if I,

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:19.872
if I drove several two or three times

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:20.973
it was probably good to go

00:07:20.973 --> 00:07:22.608
because you could cycle steel

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:23.509
hundreds of times,

00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:25.111
if not thousands of times.

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:26.779
But that's not the case with composite.

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:28.648
So it's it's quite insidious.

00:07:28.648 --> 00:07:28.948
And that,

00:07:28.948 --> 00:07:29.715
I think, lulled them

00:07:29.715 --> 00:07:31.484
into a sense of confidence

00:07:31.484 --> 00:07:33.152
and led to this tragedy.

00:07:33.152 --> 00:07:34.787
But these are known things.

00:07:34.787 --> 00:07:36.122
They're known within

00:07:36.122 --> 00:07:37.590
the engineering community and they're

00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:40.092
well, we

00:07:40.092 --> 00:07:41.561
also want to point to Ocean Gates, former

00:07:41.561 --> 00:07:43.262
director of Marine Operations.

00:07:43.262 --> 00:07:45.498
He wrote us an engineering report in 2018

00:07:45.498 --> 00:07:46.098
I think it was

00:07:46.098 --> 00:07:48.000
he focused his criticisms

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.035
on the company's decision

00:07:49.035 --> 00:07:51.037
to rely on acoustic monitoring

00:07:51.037 --> 00:07:52.505
the sounds the hull made under pressure

00:07:52.505 --> 00:07:54.674
as opposed to a a scan of the hall.

00:07:54.674 --> 00:07:55.942
According to him

00:07:55.942 --> 00:07:57.510
the company claimed no equipment existed

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:58.244
that could perform

00:07:58.244 --> 00:07:59.345
that kind of a scan

00:07:59.345 --> 00:08:00.780
on the five inch thing

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:02.715
five inch thick carbon fiber hull.

00:08:02.715 --> 00:08:03.683
I know it's

00:08:03.716 --> 00:08:04.550
difficult to say obviously

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:05.518
without reading the report,

00:08:05.518 --> 00:08:06.786
but I'm wondering what you make of that

00:08:06.786 --> 00:08:07.987
because the

00:08:08.321 --> 00:08:09.655
seems like this company

00:08:09.655 --> 00:08:10.590
was making a big deal

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:12.959
about the sensors they had that could

00:08:12.959 --> 00:08:14.393
sense a problem with the hull.

00:08:14.393 --> 00:08:15.428
And if they could sense

00:08:15.428 --> 00:08:16.062
there was a problem,

00:08:16.062 --> 00:08:17.697
then they would have time to

00:08:17.697 --> 00:08:19.465
to to turn around and go back up.

00:08:19.465 --> 00:08:21.434
Clearly, they did it.

00:08:21.434 --> 00:08:23.402
It's a bit like saying we have

00:08:23.402 --> 00:08:25.104
we have a bit of a poor design

00:08:25.104 --> 00:08:26.606
for the engine in our jet

00:08:26.606 --> 00:08:28.007
or our rocket ship.

00:08:28.007 --> 00:08:29.275
But we have a sensor that will

00:08:29.275 --> 00:08:30.643
tell us if it's on fire.

00:08:31.944 --> 00:08:33.312
To me, that's cold comfort.

00:08:33.312 --> 00:08:34.881
And I think that if you're

00:08:34.881 --> 00:08:36.148
if you're building a hall

00:08:36.148 --> 00:08:36.616
where you need

00:08:36.616 --> 00:08:37.783
to have sensors to tell you

00:08:37.783 --> 00:08:38.951
that it's failing

00:08:38.951 --> 00:08:40.553
in the process of failing,

00:08:40.553 --> 00:08:41.487
you have no business

00:08:41.487 --> 00:08:42.255
designing subs

00:08:42.255 --> 00:08:44.857
or being in that in that sub.

00:08:44.857 --> 00:08:47.360
They touted it, I believe you know,

00:08:47.426 --> 00:08:48.828
as a as a good thing,

00:08:48.828 --> 00:08:50.663
as a as a safety protocol.

00:08:50.663 --> 00:08:52.431
But I consider it a bad thing

00:08:52.431 --> 00:08:53.733
because it sheds a light

00:08:53.733 --> 00:08:54.567
directly on

00:08:54.567 --> 00:08:55.601
on the fundamental flaw

00:08:55.601 --> 00:08:57.770
of their of their design.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:08:58.704
You have to remember,

00:08:58.704 --> 00:09:00.840
the DNA of this design

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:02.375
concept goes back farther.

00:09:02.375 --> 00:09:04.510
It goes back to the quest

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:05.678
to go to the challenger

00:09:05.678 --> 00:09:07.980
deep that I was involved in, obviously.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:09.549
And there was another sub design

00:09:09.549 --> 00:09:11.817
that was competing with ours at the time

00:09:11.817 --> 00:09:13.686
that was based on a wound filament

00:09:13.686 --> 00:09:15.121
composite cylinder

00:09:15.121 --> 00:09:17.089
with two titanium caps.

00:09:17.089 --> 00:09:19.091
And I told those guys point blank,

00:09:19.292 --> 00:09:21.260
you're going to get killed in that thing.

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:23.462
And they ultimately never drove it.

00:09:23.529 --> 00:09:24.964
I literally told the guy

00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:26.365
who bought the sub

00:09:26.365 --> 00:09:27.133
when its owner,

00:09:27.133 --> 00:09:28.367
Steve Fossett, the famous

00:09:28.367 --> 00:09:30.303
billionaire, died in a plane crash

00:09:30.303 --> 00:09:31.837
and the sub was then

00:09:31.837 --> 00:09:34.106
purchased by another guy to operate it.

00:09:34.106 --> 00:09:35.041
And I told him,

00:09:35.041 --> 00:09:36.208
you're going to die down there

00:09:36.208 --> 00:09:37.310
if you dove that thing.

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:39.178
And I felt very strongly about it.

00:09:39.178 --> 00:09:40.546
I had nothing to do with Ocean Gate,

00:09:40.546 --> 00:09:41.347
and I never warned,

00:09:41.347 --> 00:09:42.348
tried to warn, Stoked

00:09:42.348 --> 00:09:43.950
and Rush of the same thing

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:44.750
because I thought

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:47.153
maybe they've solved it, you know?

00:09:47.153 --> 00:09:49.722
But I was pretty opinionated about it

00:09:50.022 --> 00:09:50.790
at the time.

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:52.892
And they had a similar idea

00:09:52.892 --> 00:09:55.127
of doing acoustic sensors, too.

00:09:55.428 --> 00:09:57.029
They call it D Lamination

00:09:57.029 --> 00:09:58.331
when water ingress starts

00:09:58.331 --> 00:09:59.398
to force the layers

00:09:59.398 --> 00:10:01.801
of the of the fibers apart.

00:10:02.101 --> 00:10:03.936
And theoretically you can hear it.

00:10:03.936 --> 00:10:04.770
I actually believe

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:05.905
they heard it with their ears,

00:10:05.905 --> 00:10:07.406
not through the sensor system

00:10:07.406 --> 00:10:09.542
in the last moments of their lives.

00:10:09.542 --> 00:10:11.477
And that's quite a horrifying prospect.

